Subject: NtSetInformationFile renames: class 10 moves the file instead of overflowing a buffer

Instincts' profile rename moves each level save from the old profile folder into the new one with the XDK's
MoveFile: NtOpenFile for DELETE, then NtSetInformationFile class 10 (FileRenameInformation). The Xbox
structure is 16 bytes: BOOLEAN ReplaceIfExists, a 32-bit RootDirectory (-3 for the DOS-device namespace) and
an OBJECT_STRING (ANSI_STRING, 32-bit Buffer) naming the target, not NT's inline WCHAR array. The save log's
rename case (0026) read it with an NT-shaped native struct: its "FileNameLength" came from past the guest
structure, a negative value skipped the MAX_PATH cap, and memcpy ran off the host stack (C0000005 on
Windows, C00000FD under Proton) right after the new folder's SaveMeta.xbx was written.

bridge_NtSetInformationFile now copies the guest structure field by field into the native
XBOX_FILE_RENAME_INFORMATION (ANSI_STRING with a host Buffer, the name NUL-terminated, a bad or unmapped
name refused with STATUS_INVALID_PARAMETER and a stderr line). xbox_NtSetInformationFile resolves the
target the way NtCreateFile does (translate_obj_path, so path confinement and root-relative names apply)
and renames through SetFileInformationByHandle(FileRenameInfo) honouring ReplaceIfExists; the POSIX
backend uses rename(2) on the handle's path. A failure logs one stderr line and returns the matching
status (name collision, path not found, access denied). A successful rename under UDATA leaves a save log
line "<target> renamed into place".

---

--- a/src/kernel/kernel.h
+++ b/src/kernel/kernel.h
@@ -234,14 +234,13 @@ typedef struct _XBOX_FILE_DISPOSITION_INFORMATION {
     BOOLEAN DeleteFile;
